;;; A derived activator that binds a relation parameter to a fact: the
;;; program loads, and the activator activates nothing, in silence. Its
;;; twin, where no run derives the activator, is
;;; `20_derived_activator_never.ein`.
;;;
;;; `r`'s parameter `?R` names a relation, `(box (?R b))`, and `mk` derives
;;; `r`'s activator from `item`'s argument, which a declared relation's
;;; argument is: any object, a fact among them (Q-M1f.41). A relation is
;;; named by a symbol, and a fact names none, so such an activator names no
;;; relation; no command says so, at the load or at a run, as none says so
;;; of an activator of another count (Q-M1f.1, Q-M1f.115).
;;;
;;; `(item (a b))` makes `mk` derive `(r (a b))` at root. The state holds it
;;; as the fact it is, and it activates nothing: no plan of `r` is compiled
;;; for it, and `r` never fires, so root holds no `hit`. The static check
;;; flags the program, since a commitment can derive an activator of `r`
;;; that root has no plan of, so nothing is claimed of its answer
;;; (Q-M1f.564).
(relation box Node :why "{?3} is in the box")
(relation hit Node :why "{?3} is hit")
(relation item Node :why "{?3} is an item")
(box (a b))
(rule r (?R)
  :match (box (?R b))
  :assert (hit z)
  :why "a box holds a {?R} of b")
(rule mk ()
  :match (item ?f)
  :assert (r ?f)
  :why "{?f} is an item, so r is asked of it")
(item (a b))
